teries — meaning all pairs of outcomes (one from A, one from B). So we take all (a - b) for a in A, b in B. A has one outcome: 1500. B has two: 3000, -3000. So differences: (1500 - 3000) = -1500, (1500 - (-3000)) = 4500. Sum = 3000. This sum is 3000. Now, if we consider the sum of (B - A), it would be (3000 - 1500) + (-3000 - 1500) = 1500 - 4500 = -3000. So the sum of differences from B to A is negative. The reason says it prefers the lottery with the more favorable sum. Since the sum of (A - B) is positive, A has a more favorable sum. Therefore, the reason would prefer A. But the sum is not a property of A or B alone — it is a comparative sum. The reason is defined to prefer the lottery that has the more favorable sum — so if the sum of (X - Y) is positive, then X is preferred. In this case, if we compute (A - B) sum = 3000 > 0, then A is preferred. So the reason would prefer A. However, the sum is not symmetric. So the decision reason, as defined, would prefer A because the sum of (A - B) is positive. Thus, the reason can be applied: it uses the sum of pairwise differences (A - B) and prefers the lottery with the more favorable sum — which is A in this case. So the reason is applicable.",
